On Jul 31, 7:08 pm, nethack newbie <nethack.new...@gmail.com> wrote:
> 1.  What are the priorities for a Barbarian in the early game?

> I've been playing a Barbarian off and on for several months now.  I
> never seem to get very far, made it to Medusa only once.  I know I
> need to change my approach, but am not sure what is most important.

> 2.  When people talk about clearing out the Mines, are they
> really clearing everything and testing all weapons/armor/items
> for BUC?  I test some stuff when it's convenient, but I get
> bored trying on that 10th pair of iron shoes  . . .  I read
> that most people haul stuff up to the Minetown altar to test
> for BUC.  Are they really hauling everything up?

Most of it, anyway, at least as a Barbarian.  (A Wizard who's
gotten to the point where he can cast identity obviously has
other options.)

In general, at least in my games, "clearing out the Mines" is a
two phased action: I'll generally get down to minetown fairly
quickly.  Going beyond that will often wait a bit.  But in all
cases, I do use my pet to determine what's safe to try on, and
try it on.  (I once got a +7 elven cloak that way, and it's
fairly frequent to get one or two +3 pieces of armor.  And
getting that AC down is probably the highest early priority.)

> 3.  Is it worth trying to keep my exp level low, letting my
> pet kill almost everything, and then buying protection in
> Minetown?  I fail at this so often, that I'm thinking it
> should only be played by Healers.

In general, it's not a good strategy; I never go for it
intentionally.  If your pet is doing all the work anyway, and
doesn't seem to be running any risks, it can work for healers or
archelogues (who can get money from identified gems and by
digging out vaults), but I'd certainly level up a Barbarian to
at least the deapth at which he is.

(My first ascension as an archelogue did reach minetown at XP1.
Not intentionally, but my pet literally never left me the time
to kill anything before dispatching it himself.  All mines
levels before minetown lit, the branch on level two, with vaults
in both levels one and two, +3 dwarven mithral on the first
level of the mines, and such, certainly helped.  To top it off,
the minetown altar was co-aligned, and my first sacrifice gave
me Grayswandir.  Sitting there at XP1, AC -11 and Grayswandir in
hand is a rather comforting feeling.)

> 4.  I'm pretty bad at ID'ing items ... even with spoilers.  If
> the item isn't ID'ed easily (writing with a wand), then I
> usually ignore it.  Potions, scrolls, rings just kind of take
> up space in my inv.  I'm thinking I should diligently try to
> ID a scroll of identification and then start ID'ing the other
> stuff that isn't easily ID'ed.  Thoughts?

Start by price id'ing.  If you find a ring at 300, uncursed, try
it on the next time there's a gang nearby.  (Just don't leave it
on if nothing happens immediately.)  Ditto at 200.  Very cheap
scrolls, and scrolls of 60 and 80, are worth keeping
immediately.  Scrolls of 100, you can usually forget about
(although you'll need some before it's over).  About the only
potions less than 300 that are interesting are the ones of
healing, and a monster will identify those for you fairly soon.
And so on.  At some point, you will use the scrolls of id
(blessed), since you'll want to definitely identify some of the
more expensive scrolls, rings and potions.  But that's something
I rarely do very early in the game.

